\section{Incremental Sharpe definition}{

Matches \code{strat_corr_augment} in \code{plan_strategy_correlation.R}:
\verb{Sharpe(equal-weight candidate + existing) - Sharpe(equal-weight existing)},
where \code{Sharpe = CAGR / (sd(r) * sqrt(periods_per_year))} (geometric CAGR,
annualised vol). Positive = candidate adds value; negative = portfolio
improves without it.
}

\section{Detection power (#496 review, .claude/rules/detection-power-required.md)}{

Computes the candidate's OWN standalone annualised Sharpe (not the
incremental one) and asks, via \code{\link[=hd_detection_power]{hd_detection_power()}}, whether the
available sample (\code{n_obs}, the number of complete overlapping
observations after alignment) is long enough to detect a Sharpe of that
size at \code{target_power}. A candidate is never admitted on a sample too
short to tell its claimed effect apart from noise -- see
\code{.claude/rules/detection-power-required.md} requirement 4.

Three distinct outcomes, corresponding to the rule's three-state
requirement:
\itemize{
\item \code{"pass"} -- own Sharpe is positive and \code{n_obs} clears the
Bonferroni-corrected requirement (\code{min_n_periods_corrected}).
\item \code{"fail"} -- own Sharpe is positive but \code{n_obs} is short of the
corrected requirement (underpowered).
\item \code{"n_a"} -- own Sharpe is zero or negative: there is no positive
effect to test for (detection-power-required.md requirement 3 --
this is a legitimate NOT-APPLICABLE state, distinct from "could not
compute").
\item \code{"na"} -- the candidate's own Sharpe itself could not be computed
(e.g. zero variance, fewer than 2 observations). This is the ONLY
detection_power outcome that can make the overall verdict
\code{"indeterminate"}.
}
}

\section{Overall verdict}{

The quantitative checks (similarity, incremental Sharpe,
diversification_ew, detection_power) vote on the overall verdict:
\itemize{
\item \code{"indeterminate"} -- at least one of the four quantitative checks
is \code{"na"} (could not be computed). NEVER collapsed with \code{"mixed"} --
see \code{.claude/rules/checks-must-distinguish-unknown.md}. \code{verdict_reason}
names which check(s).
\item \code{"admit"} -- all four quantitative checks pass.
\item \code{"reject"} -- all four quantitative checks fail (or are \code{"n_a"},
which counts as a determinate non-pass).
\item \code{"mixed"} -- everything was computable, but the checks disagree
(some pass, some fail/n_a). A determinate result -- unlike
\code{"indeterminate"}, more analysis will not change this outcome, only a
different candidate or existing portfolio would.
}
\code{diversification_gmv}, \code{crowding}, and \code{robustness} remain advisory
(reported, never voted).
}

\section{Advisory stance}{

Never calls \code{\link[base:stop]{base::stop()}} on a failing check. A strategy that fails
every quantitative check returns a tibble of \code{"fail"} rows with
\code{overall = "reject"}. Input validation errors (non-numeric inputs, empty
existing, no shared rows) do abort via \code{\link[cli:cli_abort]{cli::cli_abort()}}.
}

